    \ingroup vscodeext-how-to-csharp

    \title Select Qt for Qt Bridge for C# projects

    Building a \l {Qt Bridge for C#} project needs a Qt installation. The build
    finds it in the \c QtDir MSBuild property, which you can also set as an
    environment variable.

    The Qt Bridge for C# packages for Windows bring their own Qt, the package
    for Windows ARM64 from version 0.5. On Linux and macOS, and with earlier
    packages for Windows ARM64, you must set \c QtDir to a Qt 6 installation
    that matches the architecture of your computer. On Windows, it must be an
    MSVC build of Qt.

    \section1 Select Qt in VS Code

    If the build of a Qt Bridge for C# project would not find Qt, \QVSC asks
    you to select one when you open the project. Select \uicontrol {Select Qt}
    to do it right away.

    To select Qt later, or to select a different Qt:

    \list 1
        \li Go to \uicontrol {Command Palette}, and select
            \uicontrol {Qt Bridge C#: Select Qt Installation}.
        \li Select one of the \l {Register Qt installations}{registered Qt
            installations}. The list only shows installations that can build
            on your computer.
        \li To use a Qt installation that is not in the list, select
            \uicontrol {Browse}, and then select the installation prefix,
            the folder that contains the \c bin and \c lib folders.
    \endlist

    For Qt from Linux distribution packages, the installation prefix is
    usually \c {/usr}. You can also select the folder that contains
    \c {Qt6Config.cmake}, such as \c {/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/cmake/Qt6}.
    \QVSC accepts the same layouts as the build: \c {lib/cmake/Qt6},
    \c {lib64/cmake/Qt6} as on Fedora and openSUSE, and
    \c {lib/<multiarch triplet>/cmake/Qt6} as on Debian and Ubuntu.

    \QVSC stores the installation prefix in the
    \l {qt-csharp-settings}{Qt Dir} setting. It uses this Qt for the QML
    language server and QML Preview, and sets it as \c QtDir in new terminals
    in the \uicontrol Terminal view. Builds that you start there, such as
    \c {dotnet build}, use the same Qt.

    \section1 Use a changed Qt in open terminals

    The \uicontrol {Qt Dir} setting applies to terminals that you open after
    you change it. Terminals that are already open keep the previous
    \c QtDir, also the terminals that \uicontrol {Developer: Reload Window}
    restores. \QVSC tells you about this when you select a different Qt
    while terminals are open, and VS Code marks such terminals with a warning
    icon on the terminal tab.

    To use the changed Qt in such a terminal, hover over the icon and select
    \uicontrol {Relaunch Terminal}, or close the terminal and open a new one.
    To check the Qt that a terminal uses, run \c {echo $QtDir} on Linux and
    macOS, or \c {$env:QtDir} in PowerShell.

    To have \uicontrol {Developer: Reload Window} start new terminals, turn
    off persistent terminal sessions. This also discards the output of the
    open terminals when you reload the window:

    \code [text]
    "terminal.integrated.enablePersistentSessions": false
    \endcode

    \note A \c QtDir that your shell profile sets, for example in
    \c {~/.bashrc}, overrides the \uicontrol {Qt Dir} setting in terminals,
    because the shell profile runs after VS Code sets the environment.

    \section1 Set QtDir for other builds

    The \uicontrol {Qt Dir} setting does not reach builds that start outside
    the \uicontrol Terminal view, such as builds from C# Dev Kit or from
    Visual Studio. For them, set \c QtDir in one of the following ways:

    \list
        \li Set the \c QtDir environment variable for your user account, and
            restart VS Code. For example, on Linux add the following line to
            your shell profile:
            \code [text]
            export QtDir=~/Qt/6.11.0/gcc_64
            \endcode
            On Windows, run the following command:
            \code [text]
            setx QtDir C:\Qt\6.11.0\msvc2022_64
            \endcode
        \li Set the \c QtDir property in the project file or in a
            \c Directory.Build.props file next to it:
            \code [text]
            <PropertyGroup>
              <QtDir>C:\Qt\6.11.0\msvc2022_64</QtDir>
            </PropertyGroup>
            \endcode
            This is useful if a project needs a particular Qt version, but
            the path is specific to your computer.
    \endlist

    A \c QtDir property in the project overrides the environment variable and
    the \uicontrol {Qt Dir} setting.

    \section1 Check which Qt the build uses

    Newer versions of the Qt Bridge for C# packages show the Qt installation
    that the build uses in the build output, for example:

    \code [text]
    Qt Bridge for C#: Using Qt at 'C:\Qt\6.11.0\msvc2022_64'.
    \endcode

    If the build does not find Qt, it stops with an error that asks you to set
    \c QtDir.

    \sa {vscodeext-how-to-build-csharp.html}
        {Build and run Qt Bridge for C# applications}
*/
